[0.009, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Why did enterprise signups fall 3% in the week of 22 May 2026?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 107s over 9 steps, 30,533 tokens, 23 observations gathered, 18 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  There is no evidence available that enterprise signups specifically fell 3% in the week of 22-28 May 2026: no tool in this environment breaks signups down by an 'enterprise' segment, and the aggregate 'user signed up' event shows only ordinary day-to-day variation (25-29 per day) with no step-down beginning that week.  [posthog.list_events 003f0faa, posthog.event_trend 3ccc8ee1]\r\n  GA4 sessions actually rose slightly from the prior week to the week of 22 May, the opposite direction of a signup decline, and no channel-level breakdown was available to examine further.  [ga4.get_sessions"]
[0.000, "o", " 24ba8e09, ga4.compare_periods 3aa9c9fd]\r\n  No supporting record of a deploy, HubSpot deal loss, lead change, or Slack discussion around this period was found, so no specific cause can be named even if the premise were true.  [github.commits 521d648f, hubspot.closed_won 5c82af20, slack.search_messages 27f43abf]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  No enterprise-segmented signup metric exists in the available data  (medium)\r\n    - A daily trend of the 'user signed up' event for 8–28 May shows counts fluctuating between 25 and 29 per day with no visible step-down beginning around 22 May.  [posthog.event_trend 3ccc8ee1]\r\n    - A weekly breakdown attempted by 'plan' returned no per-plan split; the underlying weekly totals span partial buckets (3 and 4 days) at each end of the range, which are not comparable to full-week totals and cannot be used to compute a week-over-week percentage change.  [posthog.event_trend 9c119df1]\r\n  No corroborating record of a"]
[0.001, "o", " change, deploy, deal loss, or discussion around this period  (medium)\r\n    - No commits were found in acme/product, acme/web, or acme/mobile within windows spanning 10 May through 29 May.  [github.commits 521d648f, github.commits 1e7a3559, github.commits 50b81bbc]\r\n    - No HubSpot leads, contacts, or closed-won deals were recorded for 15–28 May, and no companies show a 'customer' lifecycle stage.  [hubspot.contacts 090c3c8b, hubspot.contacts 43a11157, hubspot.closed_won 5c82af20, hubspot.search c22752c3]\r\n    - No Slack messages or decisions referencing enterprise signups, a signup drop, or related discussion were found around this period.  [slack.search_messages 27f43abf, slack.search_messages b05ff5f1, slack.find_decision b2b2a84e]\r\n    - No PostHog annotations, feature flags, or GitHub features referencing 'enterprise' or 'signup' were found.  [posthog.feature_flags 418949e4, posthog.event_trend a32a4bd7, github.find_feature 43e81e95]\r\n  Website traffic rose over the same window, not fell  (low)\r\n    -"]
[0.000, "o", " GA4 sessions rose from a 15–21 May average of roughly 673/day to a 22–28 May average of roughly 694/day, the opposite direction of a signup decline.  [ga4.get_sessions 24ba8e09]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |▆▃▃█▅▆▅▆▃▆▆▅▆▆▁▆▅▃▁▆▅|  range 25–29\r\n                        2026-05-08 … 2026-05-28\r\n\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |▁██▃|  range 80–193\r\n                        2026-05-04 … 2026-05-25\r\n\r\n  sessions over time\r\n    sessions           |▅▂▄▃▃▅▅▇▅▇▇█▅▁|  range 648–715\r\n                        2026-05-15 … 2026-05-28\r\n\r\n\r\nHYPOTHESES TESTED\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  [inconclusive] A product or app deploy around 22 May broke or degraded enterprise signup flow, causing the 3% weekly fall.\r\n      No commits were found in acme/prod"]
[0.000, "o", "uct, acme/web, or acme/mobile in this window across multiple query attempts, so there is no artifact to point to as a cause. This does not confirm nothing shipped -- the tool may simply not surface commits for this range -- so the hypothesis is neither supported nor ruled out by a specific contradicting observation.\r\n  [contradicted] A marketing or sales-side event (e.g., end of a campaign, lost deals) reduced enterprise signups that week.\r\n      HubSpot returned zero leads and zero closed-won deals for the entire 15-28 May window, which is inconsistent with there being any campaign or deal-flow signal to explain a segment-specific fall; the absence itself undercuts a sales-side explanation rather than supporting one.\r\n  [inconclusive] Enterprise signups genuinely fell 3% in the week of 22 May, driven by a real, measurable segment-level movement.\r\n      No tool available exposes an 'enterprise' segment or plan property on signup events, so a 3% figure specific to that segment cannot be measured, confirmed, or"]
[0.000, "o", " refuted with the data on hand. The aggregate daily signup trend across the surrounding weeks shows only ordinary day-to-day fluctuation (25-29/day) with no visible break at 22 May.\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATIONS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  P1 Instrument signup events (or the underlying HubSpot/CRM records) with a plan or segment property (e.g., 'enterprise' vs 'self-serve') before attempting this kind of segment-level investigation again.\r\n      why: Every attempt to isolate an enterprise-specific signal — via PostHog breakdown, HubSpot filters, or GA4 conversions — returned no rows, meaning the claimed 3% movement cannot currently be measured, let alone explained.\r\n      [posthog.list_events 003f0faa, hubspot.contacts 090c3c8b, ga4.compare_periods 3aa9c9fd]\r\n  P2 Re-run the aggregate signup trend query once the trailing week's data has fully landed (past 2026-05-28 plus a few days) before drawing any conclusion about a week-over-week change.\r\n      why: The las"]
[0.000, "o", "t event recorded in the trend series was 2026-05-25, and the tool itself flags the final weekly bucket as incomplete and unsuitable for rate comparison.\r\n      [posthog.event_trend 9c119df1]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The event trend series covering late May carries a data-freshness gap: the last recorded event is 2026-05-25, three days short of the requested 2026-05-28 end, and the trailing weekly bucket is explicitly flagged as incomplete and not valid for rate comparisons.\r\n  - No tool in this toolset returns a plan- or segment-level breakdown of signups, so an 'enterprise' cohort cannot be isolated from the aggregate signup count at all; the 3% figure in the question cannot be verified or attributed to any driver with current instrumentation.\r\n  - Multiple empty results (commits, HubSpot deals/leads, Slack search) are each individually consistent with a filter or naming mismatch rather than true absence of activity, per the too"]
[0.000, "o", "l's own caveats.\r\n  - 2 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - 5 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- executive_summary[2]: Evidence only checked commits, closed-won deals, and a narrow Slack search, but the claim also asserts absence of 'HubSpot deal loss' records and 'lead change' records which were n\r\n- findings[1].claims[0]: The evidence shows zero commits but with differing windows (May15-28 for product/web, May10-29 for mobile), not a uniform 10-29 May window for all three as claimed.\r\n- findings[1].claims[3]: No evidence item searches PostHog annotations, and feature flags/github search were only for 'enterprise' or combined 'enterprise signup', not 'signup' alone, so the claim overreac\r\n- findings[2].claims[0]: The session averages (~673 and ~695) match the data"]
[0.000, "o", " exactly, but the evidence contains no signup figures, so the comparison to a signup decline is unsupported by this evidence.\r\n- ... and 1 more.\r\n  - 1 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. The observations mostly return empty result sets (no commits, no Slack discussion, no HubSpot deal or contact changes, no feature flags) and a flat aggregate signup trend around ~27/day with no clear break in the week of 22 May; there is no dataset isolating 'enterprise' signups nor any dated change to explain a 3% movement. This establishes an absence of obvious causes but not what did cause the change, and one series is flagged as stale/degraded, undermining even the description of the movemen What would settle it: A per-emitter breakdown or trend for 'enterprise signup' specifically (not just aggregate 'user signed up') to confirm the 3% fall is real and isolated to enterprise plan; A date"]
[0.000, "o", "d deploy, config, or pricing/plan change to the signup flow or enterprise plan inside the week of 22 May; A comparable non-ent\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The weekly-interval PostHog trend has partial buckets at both ends of the requested range (3 days and 4 days covered versus 7), making raw bucket totals non-comparable; only daily rates are safe to compare.\r\n  - GA4 channel-level comparison and HubSpot company/lead/deal queries all returned zero rows for the window in question, which may reflect missing connector data for this tenant/period rather than true zero activity.\r\n  - 14 of the 18 cited observation(s) returned no results (ga4.compare_periods, github.commits, github.find_feature, hubspot.closed_won, hubspot.contacts, hubspot.search, posthog.event_trend, posthog.feature_flags, slack.find_decision, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOU"]
